

# Commit Message
#     - Format
#         - Subject line
#         - Blank line
#         - Body
#         - Blank line
#         - Footer
#     - Subject line
#         - <type>(<scope>): <subject>
#         - type is one of
#             - feat    : A new feature
#             - fix     : A bug fix
#             - docs    : Documentation only changes
#             - style   : Changes that do not affect the meaning of the code
#                         (white-space, formatting, missing semi-colons, etc)
#             - refactor: A code change that neither fixes a bug nor adds a feature
#             - perf    : A code change that improves performance
#             - test    : Adding missing or correcting existing tests
#             - chore   : Changes to the build process or auxiliary tools and
#                         libraries such as documentation generation
#         - scope specifies which component is affected
#             - e.g. ai, games, docs
#             - * can be used to indicate more than one scope
#         - subject is a short description
#             - use imperative present tense
#                 - e.g. change not changed or changes
#             - do not capitalize
#             - no . at end
#         - max length
#             - recommended <= 50 characters
#             - max 72 characters
#             - readable in git log --oneline
#             - allows for use as reference/footnote
#     - Blank line
#     - Body
#         - Explain what and why, not how
#         - Wrap at 72 characters
#     - Blank line
#     - Footer
#         - links to issues (optional)
#         - links to relevant commits
#             - shorthash (subject)
#             - e.g.: 523cb73 (feat(logs): add resource usage and temp logs)
#             - use git ref alias below for the correct format
#             - git config alias.ref "log --format='%h (%s)' -1"
#             - git ref <hash>
#         - other footnotes
